Hello S-users,

I am trying to build a glm model using Gordon Smyth's "tariff" function.
The function was specifically written to build double glm's (modeling mean
and dispersion simultaneously) for insurance data.  Basically, after
appropriate settings for the mean model and the dispersion model, the
program runs an iteration of the mean model, an iteration of the dispersion
model, then calculates a combined likelihood and keeps alternating between
mean and dispersion's iterations untill the convergence criterion is met.
I have used this function quite extensively in the past with little
problems.

This time when I try to run a preliminary model with a three variable
interaction the likelihood goes in the right direction for a few iterations
after which it starts behaving strangely: going up a little, then a little
further down than the previous down step, then up again and so on; on each
down step it goes down a little more than the previous down step??  With
this behaviour I feel it'll never converge?

"sexeprin" is a two level factor, the other two variables are continuous
and take values between 0 and 100.  I tried a few different formulations of
my interaction terms and I observed the same type of behaviour.  With
"sexeprin" removed the function converges in a few iterations.  The process
is pretty lengthty as the input data set has over 2M records.

