From stefano.sofia at usa.net  Wed Jun  4 10:23:50 2003
From: stefano.sofia at usa.net (Stefano Sofia)
Date: Wed, 04 Jun 2003 11:23:50 +0100
Subject: always about rejection sampling
Message-ID: <089HFDkXY6288S01.1054722230@uwdvg001.cms.usa.net>

Dear Splus users,
few weeks ago I posted a question about rejection sampling, 
and suggestions offered by Dr.Ripley have been useful. 
Unfortunately it is not time, yet, to post a valid summary of the solutions to
my issue;
I am still working on finding a good envelope function, but unfortunately I am
still encountering some numerical problem.

g(x) = N(mu,sigma^(2)) * exp(alpha*x)/(1 + exp(alpha*x)) 
is always smaller than
G(x) = N(mu,sigma^(2))*exp(alpha*x)
and it is also true that (I checked it carefully, believe me) 
G can be written as
G(x) = N(mu + alpha*sigma^(2),sigma^(2))*k
where 
k = exp(alpha^(2) * sigma^(2)/2 + alpha*mu)

Then,
sampled a value Y1 from 
N(mu + alpha*sigma^(2),sigma^(2))
I should always have that
g(Y1) < G(Y1).
The below reported procedure does exactly what I described; with initial
parameters
(-62.6865,0.000025,1)
sometimes it works correctly, sometimes not (set digits to 17 in order to see
the full length of double precision numbers).
Any idea of this behaviour? Why sometimes g(Y1) > G(Y1)?

hope you can help me
thank you for your usual attention
Stefano

function(normalmean, normalvariance, state)
{
alpha <- 1
newnormalmean <- (normalmean + alpha * normalvariance)
k <- exp((alpha^(2) * normalvariance)/2 + alpha * normalmean)
Y1 <- rnorm(1, mean = newnormalmean, sd = sqrt(normalvariance))
bigGY1 <- dnorm(Y1, mean = newnormalmean, sd = sqrt(normalvariance)) * k
smallgY1 <- (dnorm(Y1, mean = normalmean, sd = sqrt(normalvariance)) *
exp(alpha * Y1))/(1 + exp(alpha * Y1))
if(smallgY1 > bigGY1) {
        print("fatal error : g(Y1) > G(Y1)")
        break
}
result <- list(smallgY1, bigGY1)
result
}

