From zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu  Fri Jun  6 21:57:48 2003
From: zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu (Alan Zaslavsky)
Date: Fri, 06 Jun 2003 17:57:48 -0400
Subject: Re: Subscripting
In-Reply-To: <000001c32c19$2cfc1b70$37997bd5@r850e>
Message-ID: <200306062157.RAA04801@nightingale.hcp.med.harvard.edu>

> Looks like a precision issue - your problem is highlighted by the
> following example .
>  
> > (2 - 1) == 1
> [1] T
> > (2.1 - 1.1) == 1
> [1] T
> > (2.11 - 1.11) == 1
> [1] F
> > (2.111 - 1.111) == 1
> [1] F
>  
> You could use zapsmall (or round) to get around this problem in the
> short term .
>  
> > zapsmall(6.13 - 4.48) == 1.65
> [1] T

For those who are curious, here is what is happening here:  A floating
point number is represented as a binary fraction with a fixed number of
places and a 1 in the first place, times a power of 2.  For a number 2.xx
the place value of the first place is twice that with 1.xx.  Hence there
is one less bit of precision at the right end.  If the last bit on the
right end of 1.xx happens to be a 0, dropping it doesn't change the value
of the fractional part, so the subtraction gives an exact 1.  If the last
bit happens to be a 1, then the subtraction does not give an exact 1.

Moral: never treat floating point calculations as exact, if it will make
a difference.  FIgure out how much error is likely (not hard for simple
expressions like x-y) and deal with it accordingly.

