Steve,

This is with respect to the theoretical part.

Let's just look at the special case in your example: 
  p=rep(.25,4), n=3, K=4, size=2
  
  Let (X1,X1) be the two numbers randomly picked from (1,2,3,4)
  N=min(X1,X2)    
  If you focus on the each row of A, you will see that 
     P(N=1)=P((1,*) or (*,1))=7/16
     P(N=2)=P((2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(4,2))=5/16
     P(N=3)=P((3,3),(3,4),(4,3))=3/16
     P(N=4)=P(4,4))=1/16
  As these rows are independent, the colSums of A is distributed 
  as MN(3,p=(7,5,3,1)/16)

  Analogously, the colSums of B is distributed as 
  MN(3,p=(1,3,5,7)/16), since this time you are looking 
  backwardly.

As size goes up, this problem gets more complicated. However,
it still should be computationally manageable.

Hope it is correct.

jingshan

