Subject: Re: Using nonlinear least square regression
In-Reply-To: <CC189F10F7F6F744B96880AC96CBFA0E20215D@edc.gsph.pitt.edu>
Message-ID: <CC189F10F7F6F744B96880AC96CBFA0E202163@edc.gsph.pitt.edu>

 

I just wanted to thank you for the valuable inputs.  I was able to 
fit the

model yesterday by rewriting my Lambda1, Lambda2 and A as functions 
of

'Delta'.   And then call them in the NLS( ) function. S plus 
liked it very much.

 

Just to give you a synopsis of this study,  we are trying to 
understand

the viral kinetics of Hepatitis C virus with our new treatment 
regimen

(pegylated interferon plus ribavirin) over time.  and that there are 
these

mathematical models, which derived 
from differential equations by some mathematicians,

presumably  fit the biphasic decline of Hep C viral 
load.

 

The messy equation is their second solution, and estimates the 
elimination rate

of the hep C infected cells, which is the 'Delta'.   E is 
efficacy of the treatment,

CL is clearance rate of virions. E and CL were estimated from 
another solution 

previously.  Oh, I'm getting into too much detail now. Sorry.  
And here are the codes.

 

 

lam1 <- function(Delta) { 0.5*(( CL+Delta )  + ( ( CL-Delta )^2 + 
(4*(1-E)*CL*Delta) ) ^(1/2) ) }
lam2 <- function(Delta) { 0.5*(( CL+Delta 
)  - ( ( CL-Delta )^2 + (4*(1-E)*CL*Delta) ) ^(1/2) ) }

theA     <- function(Delta) { 
   
((E*CL -(0.5*(( CL+Delta )-((CL-Delta)^2 + (4*(1-E)*CL*Delta))^(1/2)))) / 

   ((CL-Delta )^2 + (4*(1-E)*CL*Delta) ) ^(1/2) )
    
}

param(new.data,"Delta") <- 0.05 

Deltafit.log10 <- nls(log10(vl) ~ 
log10(v0*(theA(Delta)*exp(-lam1(Delta)*(time-t0))+(1-theA(Delta))*exp(-lam2(Delta)*(time-t0)))  
)
,data="" trace=T)

 

 

Thank you!

 

kelly

 

 

 

