Bernd,

I guess the reason you got error message from my code is
because the code can not be run for the second time. 
Here is why. Let's create a data set:

 id <- sample(1:3,10,T)
 id<- sort(id)
 x1<- rle(id)
 x2<- sample(1:100, length(x1$values), replace=F)
 x<- rep(NA, length(id))
 x[c(1,cumsum(x1$lengths[-length(x1$lengths)])+1)]<-x2
 data<- as.data.frame(cbind(id, x))
> data
   id  x 
 1  1 15
 2  1 NA
 3  1 NA
 4  2 24
 5  2 NA
 6  2 NA
 7  2 NA
 8  2 NA
 9  3 46
10  3 NA

Note that *data$x only has non-missing values for the first entry of
each unique id*. This is quite common in medical data when the next
visit value for the same patient remains the same as the previous visit
(like gender, race code), the same data is often not entered each time
in the follow-up visits.

Now you run my code to lag down those values:
 data<- sort.col(data, c(1,2), ascending=T)
 x1<- rle(data$id)
 x<- data$x[is.na(data$x)==F]
 data$x<- rep(x, times=x1$lengths)
> data
   id  x 
 1  1 15
 2  1 15
 3  1 15
 4  2 24
 5  2 24
 6  2 24
 7  2 24
 8  2 24
 9  3 46
10  3 46

However, if you run the same code again, you will get error
message. This is because the condition  *data$x only has non-missing
values for the first entry of each unique id*
does not hold and command
       x<- data$x[is.na(data$x)==F]
will give you different answer as that from the first time.
Hope it helps.
Peng

