Let F(x) = .999 phi(x) + .001 G(x), where G is the cdf which puts mass
1  at 1 million. phi is the std normal cdf. F and phi are
"close", depending of course on how "closeness" is
measured.

 Let X be a std. normal cdf with prob .999 and be 1 million with
prob. 0.001.    X has cdf F(x). 

.

For small samples from F (say < 100) the sample is unlikely to contain
the 1 million, and the sample mean will be small . However, for n = k
(1000), with k not close to zero, the probability that the sample with
contain the 1 million is approximately 1 - exp(-k) , and the mean will be
at least (1 million)/(1000 k) = 1000/k. 

Jim Stapleton

